    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. can you go into more detail on the screen reader stuff? specifically about aria labels and how to handle dynamic content updates so the screen reader actually picks up the changes in real-time.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Of course, let's delve deeper into the enchanted forest of screen reader compatibility, where aria labels are the guiding lights and dynamic content updates are the ever-changing scenery.\n\n1. **Aria Labels**: These are like the magical runes that guide the screen reader through your app's content. Use `aria-label`, `aria-labelledby`, and `aria-describedby` to provide descriptive labels for elements that might not be inherently descriptive. For example, if you have a button that performs a specific action, like \"Submit Payment,\" you can use `aria-label=\"Submit Payment\"` to make it clear to screen reader users what the button does.\n\n2. **Handling Dynamic Content Updates**: Dynamic content can be tricky, like a mischievous goblin that changes the scenery without warning. To ensure screen readers pick up these changes in real-time, use ARIA live regions. These are special areas in your app that can be marked with `aria-live=\"polite\"` or `aria-live=\"assertive\"`. The \"polite\" setting will announce changes when the user is not busy, while \"assertive\" will interrupt the current task to announce the change immediately. For example, if you have a stock ticker that updates with new prices, you",
      "source": "target",
